{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,21]],"date-time":"2026-09-21T11:34:50Z","timestamp":1789990490798,"version":"4.0.1"},"reference-count":31,"publisher":"Wiley","issue":"28","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2014,5,28]],"date-time":"2014-05-28T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1401235200000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/onlinelibrary.wiley.com\/termsAndConditions#vor"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["Angewandte Chemie"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2014,7,7]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title><jats:p>An iridium oxide nanoparticle electrocatalyst under oxygen evolution reaction conditions was probed in\u2005situ by ambient\u2010pressure X\u2010ray photoelectron spectroscopy. Under OER conditions, iridium undergoes a change in oxidation state from Ir<jats:sup>IV<\/jats:sup> to Ir<jats:sup>V<\/jats:sup> that takes place predominantly at the surface of the catalyst. The chemical change in iridium is coupled to a decrease in surface hydroxide, providing experimental evidence which strongly suggests that the oxygen evolution reaction on iridium oxide occurs through an OOH\u2010mediated deprotonation mechanism.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1002\/ange.201402311","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2014,5,28]],"date-time":"2014-05-28T19:32:15Z","timestamp":1401305535000},"page":"7297-7300","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":80,"title":["In\u2005Situ Observation of Surface Species on Iridium Oxide Nanoparticles during the Oxygen Evolution Reaction"],"prefix":"10.1002","volume":"126","author":[{"given":"Hernan G.","family":"Sanchez\u2005Casalongue","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"May Ling","family":"Ng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sarp","family":"Kaya","seque